<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orkney</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orkney Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orkney is an archipelago of some seventy islands off the north-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, across the Pentland Firth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>. It holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> monuments such as Skara Brae and Maeshowe, and was a Pictish land before Norse settlers made it the seat of the earldom of Orkney in the 9th century. Pledged by the Danish-Norwegian crown to Scotland in 1468, it passed to the Scottish crown in 1472.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orkney.jpg" alt="Orkney" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rab Knight from en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2007_Flag_of_Orkney.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Áedán mac Gabráin, who according to Adomnán was ordained king by Columba, campaigned widely: the annals record his expedition to <b>Orkney</b> around 580 and a battle around 582 in Manann, either the Isle of Man or Manaw on the Forth, and Adomnán describes his battle against the Miathi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The overseas realm was tested in 1263, when Haakon IV led a great fleet to defend the Hebrides; after the inconclusive Battle of Largs he died in <b>Orkney</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Galloway, Moray and the Norse-ruled north and west were brought under royal control only slowly: after the Battle of Largs in 1263, the Treaty of Perth of 1266 transferred the Western Isles and the Isle of Man from Norway to Scotland, and <b>Orkney</b> and Shetland followed in 1468–1469.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pictish kings also had fleets: the Irish annals record that Bridei son of Beli attacked the <b>Orkneys</b> in 682, and that 150 Pictish ships were wrecked in 729.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse kings ruled Dublin, founded as a Viking base in 841, and the Isle of Man, and in <b>Orkney</b> and Shetland a descendant of Old Norse, Norn, was spoken until the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse settlers took <b>Orkney</b>, Shetland, the Hebrides and the Isle of Man.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
